1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,892
In the winter of 1913, a thick envelope
arrived at Trinity College, Cambridge.

2
00:00:05,892 --> 00:00:08,752
It was addressed to Godfrey Harold Hardy,

3
00:00:08,752 --> 00:00:11,821
one of the finest mathematicians in England.

4
00:00:11,821 --> 00:00:15,170
The postmark was from
Madras, in southern India.

5
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:21,268
Inside were page after page of formulas,
written in a careful, cramped hand.

6
00:00:21,268 --> 00:00:22,878
Some were already known.

7
00:00:22,878 --> 00:00:24,018
A few were wrong.

8
00:00:24,018 --> 00:00:27,507
But others were unlike
anything Hardy had ever seen.

9
00:00:28,507 --> 00:00:30,356
They defeated me completely.

10
00:00:30,356 --> 00:00:34,055
I had never seen anything in
the least like them before.

11
00:00:34,055 --> 00:00:37,358
They must be true, because,
if they were not true,

12
00:00:37,358 --> 00:00:40,859
no one would have had the
imagination to invent them.

13
00:00:42,659 --> 00:00:44,979
The man who sent them had no degree.

14
00:00:44,979 --> 00:00:47,299
He had failed out of college, twice.

15
00:00:47,299 --> 00:00:51,618
He was a clerk in a shipping office,
earning thirty rupees a month.

16
00:00:51,618 --> 00:00:53,744
His name was Srinivasa Ramanujan.

17
00:00:54,744 --> 00:00:59,457
Within five years, he would be one of
the most celebrated mathematicians in the

18
00:00:59,457 --> 00:00:59,815
world.

19
00:00:59,815 --> 00:01:03,811
And within seven years, he would
be dead, at the age of thirty-two.

20
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:25,179
Srinivasa Ramanujan was born on
the twenty-second of December,

21
00:01:25,179 --> 00:01:31,098
1887, in the town of Erode, in what was
then the Madras Presidency of British India.

22
00:01:31,098 --> 00:01:33,845
He was born in his grandmother's house,

23
00:01:33,845 --> 00:01:39,482
but he grew up in Kumbakonam, an ancient
temple town in the fertile delta of the

24
00:01:39,482 --> 00:01:40,398
Kaveri river.

25
00:01:41,398 --> 00:01:45,351
His family were Tamil Brahmins:
devout, respected, and poor.

26
00:01:45,351 --> 00:01:49,041
His father worked as a clerk
in a cloth merchant's shop.

27
00:01:49,041 --> 00:01:53,521
His mother, Komalatammal, sang
devotional songs at the local temple.

28
00:01:53,521 --> 00:01:58,265
She was a strong-willed woman, and
the dominant force in her son's life.

29
00:01:59,265 --> 00:02:04,549
The family lived in a small, single-storey
house on Sarangapani Sannidhi Street,

30
00:02:04,549 --> 00:02:07,190
in the shadow of the great temple tower.

31
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:08,709
The house still stands.

32
00:02:08,709 --> 00:02:10,096
Today it is a museum.

33
00:02:11,096 --> 00:02:13,256
Life in Kumbakonam was fragile.

34
00:02:13,256 --> 00:02:17,089
When he was two years old,
Ramanujan survived smallpox.

35
00:02:17,089 --> 00:02:21,827
Several of his younger brothers and
sisters did not survive infancy.

36
00:02:22,827 --> 00:02:25,091
At school, his gift appeared early.

37
00:02:25,091 --> 00:02:30,265
By the age of eleven, he had exhausted
the mathematical knowledge of two college

38
00:02:30,265 --> 00:02:32,594
students who lodged with his family.

39
00:02:32,594 --> 00:02:36,604
At thirteen, he mastered an
advanced textbook on trigonometry,

40
00:02:36,604 --> 00:02:39,320
and began to discover theorems of his own.

41
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:45,018
Classmates remembered a quiet boy who
seemed to live in a world of numbers.

42
00:02:45,018 --> 00:02:49,027
He could recite the digits of pi,
and of the square root of two,

43
00:02:49,027 --> 00:02:51,658
to as many places as anyone cared to hear.

44
00:02:51,658 --> 00:02:56,231
Then, at the age of sixteen, a book
came into his hands that would change

45
00:02:56,231 --> 00:02:56,920
everything.

46
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:09,743
It was called A Synopsis of Elementary
Results in Pure and Applied Mathematics,

47
00:03:09,743 --> 00:03:13,315
by George Shoobridge Carr,
a private tutor in London.

48
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:14,932
It was not a great book.

49
00:03:14,932 --> 00:03:18,705
It was a list: some five
thousand theorems and formulas,

50
00:03:18,705 --> 00:03:24,028
set down with little or no proof, meant
to help students cram for examinations.

51
00:03:25,028 --> 00:03:27,123
For Ramanujan, it was a doorway.

52
00:03:27,123 --> 00:03:32,426
With no teacher to guide him, he took each
bare result and worked out for himself

53
00:03:32,426 --> 00:03:33,473
why it was true.

54
00:03:33,473 --> 00:03:34,848
Then he went further.

55
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:39,365
He began to record his own discoveries:
formulas for infinite series,

56
00:03:39,365 --> 00:03:43,817
for continued fractions, for the
hidden properties of whole numbers.

57
00:03:44,817 --> 00:03:47,671
Paper was expensive, so
he worked on a slate,

58
00:03:47,671 --> 00:03:50,462
rubbing out his calculations with his elbow,

59
00:03:50,462 --> 00:03:53,824
and copied only the final
results into his notebooks.

60
00:03:53,824 --> 00:03:57,186
It was a habit that would
shape the rest of his life.

61
00:03:57,186 --> 00:04:01,182
Ramanujan wrote down what he
found, but rarely how he found it.

62
00:04:02,182 --> 00:04:04,122
His passion came at a price.

63
00:04:04,122 --> 00:04:08,971
In 1904, he won a scholarship to the
Government College in Kumbakonam.

64
00:04:08,971 --> 00:04:11,673
But he could think only of mathematics.

65
00:04:11,673 --> 00:04:17,423
He neglected his other subjects, failed
his examinations, and lost the scholarship.

66
00:04:17,423 --> 00:04:20,817
Ashamed, he ran away from
home for several weeks.

67
00:04:21,817 --> 00:04:26,127
Two years later, he tried again,
at Pachaiyappa's College in Madras.

68
00:04:26,127 --> 00:04:28,473
Once more he excelled in mathematics.

69
00:04:28,473 --> 00:04:31,198
Once more he failed nearly everything else.

70
00:04:31,198 --> 00:04:32,783
He left without a degree.

71
00:04:33,783 --> 00:04:37,641
For the next five years, Ramanujan
lived on the edge of poverty.

72
00:04:37,641 --> 00:04:41,439
He tutored students when he could,
and at times he went hungry.

73
00:04:41,439 --> 00:04:43,971
All the while, the notebooks kept growing.

74
00:04:44,971 --> 00:04:49,774
In 1909, following custom, his mother
arranged his marriage to Janaki,

75
00:04:49,774 --> 00:04:50,734
a girl of ten.

76
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:56,360
As was usual at the time, she remained
with her own family for several more years.

77
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:00,888
Now, with a household to support,
Ramanujan went looking for work,

78
00:05:00,888 --> 00:05:04,387
carrying his notebooks from
one office to the next.

79
00:05:05,387 --> 00:05:08,482
In 1910, he found his
way to Ramachandra Rao,

80
00:05:08,482 --> 00:05:13,158
a senior government official and a
passionate patron of mathematics.

81
00:05:13,158 --> 00:05:16,734
Rao was sceptical of the
shabbily dressed young man.

82
00:05:16,734 --> 00:05:21,754
But as Ramanujan led him through
elliptic integrals and divergent series,

83
00:05:21,754 --> 00:05:24,230
his doubts gave way to astonishment.

84
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:28,494
He agreed to support Ramanujan
with a small monthly allowance.

85
00:05:29,494 --> 00:05:32,729
In 1911, Ramanujan
published his first paper,

86
00:05:32,729 --> 00:05:37,907
on Bernoulli numbers, in the Journal
of the Indian Mathematical Society.

87
00:05:37,907 --> 00:05:43,515
And in March 1912, he found a steady
job: an accounts clerk at the Madras Port

88
00:05:43,515 --> 00:05:45,816
Trust, at thirty rupees a month.

89
00:05:46,816 --> 00:05:50,083
His superiors, including
the Port Trust's chairman,

90
00:05:50,083 --> 00:05:53,414
Sir Francis Spring, quietly
encouraged his research.

91
00:05:53,414 --> 00:05:56,168
Ramanujan finished his office work quickly,

92
00:05:56,168 --> 00:05:59,114
and spent the rest of
his time on mathematics.

93
00:05:59,114 --> 00:06:04,430
But to be truly understood, his work needed
the attention of the experts in Europe.

94
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:17,337
With the help of his supporters, Ramanujan
wrote to leading mathematicians in

95
00:06:17,337 --> 00:06:17,898
Britain.

96
00:06:17,898 --> 00:06:20,005
The replies were discouraging.

97
00:06:20,005 --> 00:06:21,830
One offered polite advice.

98
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:24,709
Others returned his work without comment.

99
00:06:24,709 --> 00:06:28,570
Then, on the sixteenth of
January, 1913, he wrote to G.

100
00:06:28,570 --> 00:06:28,711
H.

101
00:06:28,711 --> 00:06:30,045
Hardy at Cambridge.

102
00:06:31,045 --> 00:06:37,145
Dear Sir, I beg to introduce myself to you
as a clerk in the Accounts Department of

103
00:06:37,145 --> 00:06:42,804
the Port Trust Office at Madras, on a
salary of only twenty pounds per annum.

104
00:06:42,804 --> 00:06:48,978
I have had no university education, but I
have undergone the ordinary school course.

105
00:06:48,978 --> 00:06:54,931
I have not trodden through the conventional
regular course which is followed in a

106
00:06:54,931 --> 00:06:59,561
university course, but I am
striking out a new path for myself.

107
00:07:01,361 --> 00:07:04,307
Hardy was thirty-five, a Fellow of Trinity,

108
00:07:04,307 --> 00:07:06,911
and a champion of rigour: the careful,

109
00:07:06,911 --> 00:07:11,090
step-by-step proof that is the
bedrock of modern mathematics.

110
00:07:11,090 --> 00:07:15,544
Strangers often sent him papers
claiming to solve great problems.

111
00:07:15,544 --> 00:07:17,531
Most were the work of cranks.

112
00:07:17,531 --> 00:07:19,860
At first, he set the letter aside.

113
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:23,717
But the formulas would not leave him alone.

114
00:07:23,717 --> 00:07:29,299
There were strange infinite series, and
continued fractions: fractions nested inside

115
00:07:29,299 --> 00:07:34,549
fractions, without end, that somehow
collapsed into exact and beautiful values.

116
00:07:35,549 --> 00:07:40,454
That evening, Hardy took the letter to
his colleague and closest collaborator,

117
00:07:40,454 --> 00:07:41,963
John Edensor Littlewood.

118
00:07:41,963 --> 00:07:45,673
By the end of the night, the
two men had reached a verdict.

119
00:07:45,673 --> 00:07:46,994
This was not a fraud.

120
00:07:46,994 --> 00:07:48,754
It was the work of a genius.

121
00:07:49,754 --> 00:07:53,003
Some of the results were
already known in Europe.

122
00:07:53,003 --> 00:07:54,329
A few were mistaken.

123
00:07:54,329 --> 00:07:58,505
But those that were new revealed
a mind of extraordinary power,

124
00:07:58,505 --> 00:08:00,030
working entirely alone.

125
00:08:00,030 --> 00:08:01,687
Hardy wrote back at once.

126
00:08:01,687 --> 00:08:03,013
He asked for proofs.

127
00:08:03,013 --> 00:08:07,720
And he began to plan something bolder:
to bring Ramanujan to Cambridge.

128
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:16,687
There was an obstacle.

129
00:08:16,687 --> 00:08:19,489
For an orthodox Brahmin, crossing the sea,

130
00:08:19,489 --> 00:08:24,826
the kala pani, or black water, meant
losing caste: being cut off from family and

131
00:08:24,826 --> 00:08:25,493
community.

132
00:08:25,493 --> 00:08:29,830
At first, Ramanujan refused, and
his mother would not hear of it.

133
00:08:30,830 --> 00:08:33,979
Early in 1914, Hardy's
colleague Eric Neville,

134
00:08:33,979 --> 00:08:37,881
visiting Madras to lecture,
pressed the invitation again.

135
00:08:37,881 --> 00:08:39,934
This time, the answer changed.

136
00:08:39,934 --> 00:08:45,479
According to family accounts, Ramanujan's
mother dreamed that the family goddess,

137
00:08:45,479 --> 00:08:50,203
Namagiri, commanded her not to stand
in the way of her son's destiny.

138
00:08:51,203 --> 00:08:55,372
Ramanujan cut his long hair,
learned to wear Western clothes,

139
00:08:55,372 --> 00:08:57,970
and on the seventeenth of March, 1914,

140
00:08:57,970 --> 00:09:00,841
he boarded the steamship Nevasa at Madras.

141
00:09:00,841 --> 00:09:04,805
He left behind his mother, his
father, and his young wife.

142
00:09:04,805 --> 00:09:07,744
Nearly a month later, he arrived in London.

143
00:09:08,744 --> 00:09:11,332
Neville met him and took him to Cambridge.

144
00:09:11,332 --> 00:09:14,043
Soon he moved into rooms at Trinity College,

145
00:09:14,043 --> 00:09:17,185
in Whewell's Court, a few
minutes' walk from Hardy.

146
00:09:17,185 --> 00:09:22,175
He had arrived at one of the great centres
of mathematical learning in the world.

147
00:09:22,175 --> 00:09:25,256
In less than four months,
Britain would be at war.

148
00:09:32,956 --> 00:09:37,937
The partnership that followed is one of
the most remarkable in the history of

149
00:09:37,937 --> 00:09:38,454
science.

150
00:09:38,454 --> 00:09:40,719
Hardy and Ramanujan were opposites.

151
00:09:40,719 --> 00:09:43,177
Hardy was an atheist, a cricket lover,

152
00:09:43,177 --> 00:09:46,541
a rationalist who trusted
only what could be proved.

153
00:09:46,541 --> 00:09:48,546
Ramanujan was deeply religious.

154
00:09:48,546 --> 00:09:53,527
He is said to have remarked that an
equation had no meaning for him unless it

155
00:09:53,527 --> 00:09:55,274
expressed a thought of God.

156
00:09:56,274 --> 00:10:01,701
Hardy soon discovered that Ramanujan's
knowledge was as patchy as it was profound.

157
00:10:01,701 --> 00:10:06,533
Working alone, he had rediscovered
results that had taken Europe's finest

158
00:10:06,533 --> 00:10:08,916
mathematicians a century to develop.

159
00:10:08,916 --> 00:10:13,748
Yet he had never learned some of the
most basic ideas of modern analysis.

160
00:10:14,748 --> 00:10:19,746
The limitations of his knowledge
were as startling as its profundity.

161
00:10:19,746 --> 00:10:25,178
Here was a man who could work out
modular equations and theorems of complex

162
00:10:25,178 --> 00:10:27,858
multiplication, to orders unheard of;

163
00:10:27,858 --> 00:10:33,652
and yet he had never heard of a doubly
periodic function or of Cauchy's theorem,

164
00:10:33,652 --> 00:10:39,519
and had indeed but the vaguest idea of
what a function of a complex variable was.

165
00:10:41,319 --> 00:10:46,006
Hardy faced a delicate task: to teach
Ramanujan the discipline of proof,

166
00:10:46,006 --> 00:10:49,001
without breaking the
spell of his inspiration.

167
00:10:49,001 --> 00:10:50,368
They found a balance.

168
00:10:50,368 --> 00:10:55,316
Ramanujan brought the ideas, sometimes
half a dozen new theorems in a single

169
00:10:55,316 --> 00:10:55,836
morning.

170
00:10:55,836 --> 00:10:58,506
Hardy and Littlewood helped to test them,

171
00:10:58,506 --> 00:11:02,932
to prove them, and to set them in
a form the world could understand.

172
00:11:03,932 --> 00:11:07,356
Consider one of their
great problems: partitions.

173
00:11:07,356 --> 00:11:12,038
A partition is a way of writing a
number as a sum of whole numbers.

174
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:15,531
The number four can be
written in five ways: four;

175
00:11:15,531 --> 00:11:16,579
three plus one;

176
00:11:16,579 --> 00:11:17,488
two plus two;

177
00:11:17,488 --> 00:11:19,025
two plus one plus one;

178
00:11:19,025 --> 00:11:21,470
and one plus one plus one plus one.

179
00:11:22,470 --> 00:11:25,179
As numbers grow, the partitions explode.

180
00:11:25,179 --> 00:11:26,398
Ten has forty-two.

181
00:11:26,398 --> 00:11:30,258
One hundred has more than one
hundred and ninety million.

182
00:11:30,258 --> 00:11:32,764
Two hundred has nearly four trillion.

183
00:11:32,764 --> 00:11:35,269
Counting them one by one is hopeless.

184
00:11:36,269 --> 00:11:39,697
So Hardy and Ramanujan
set out to find a formula.

185
00:11:39,697 --> 00:11:43,335
In 1918, they published it:
a remarkable expression,

186
00:11:43,335 --> 00:11:47,392
built from pi, square roots,
and the exponential function.

187
00:11:47,392 --> 00:11:53,198
To test it, their colleague Percy MacMahon
worked out the partitions of two hundred

188
00:11:53,198 --> 00:11:53,758
by hand.

189
00:11:53,758 --> 00:11:58,795
Using just a handful of terms, their
formula landed on the exact answer,

190
00:11:58,795 --> 00:12:00,613
correct to the last digit.

191
00:12:01,613 --> 00:12:05,275
Along the way, they created
a powerful new technique,

192
00:12:05,275 --> 00:12:09,145
the circle method, which
mathematicians still use today.

193
00:12:09,145 --> 00:12:12,807
Ramanujan also noticed
patterns no one had suspected.

194
00:12:12,807 --> 00:12:18,611
Take any number ending in four or nine: its
number of partitions is always divisible

195
00:12:18,611 --> 00:12:19,163
by five.

196
00:12:19,163 --> 00:12:22,480
Similar patterns hold for
seven, and for eleven.

197
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:24,826
Then there was pi.

198
00:12:24,826 --> 00:12:29,762
In 1914, Ramanujan published
seventeen new series for one over pi.

199
00:12:29,762 --> 00:12:35,521
The most famous of them converges with
breathtaking speed: each new term adds

200
00:12:35,521 --> 00:12:37,690
roughly eight correct digits.

201
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:43,673
Seventy years later, in 1985, it was used
to calculate pi to more than seventeen

202
00:12:43,673 --> 00:12:44,795
million digits.

203
00:12:45,795 --> 00:12:51,370
In March 1916, Cambridge awarded Ramanujan
a Bachelor of Arts degree by research,

204
00:12:51,370 --> 00:12:54,055
the forerunner of the modern doctorate,

205
00:12:54,055 --> 00:12:58,323
for his work on highly composite
numbers: numbers like twelve,

206
00:12:58,323 --> 00:13:04,105
sixty, and three hundred and sixty, that
have more divisors than any smaller number.

207
00:13:04,105 --> 00:13:08,579
The man who had twice failed
college now held a Cambridge degree.

208
00:13:16,279 --> 00:13:18,153
But Cambridge was not home.

209
00:13:18,153 --> 00:13:20,581
The war had emptied the university.

210
00:13:20,581 --> 00:13:23,495
Littlewood left to serve in the artillery.

211
00:13:23,495 --> 00:13:26,826
Colleges became barracks
and military hospitals.

212
00:13:26,826 --> 00:13:30,295
And the English winters
were long, dark, and cold.

213
00:13:31,295 --> 00:13:35,824
Ramanujan was a strict vegetarian who
cooked his own meals in his rooms.

214
00:13:35,824 --> 00:13:40,039
With wartime shortages, the foods
he depended on were hard to find.

215
00:13:40,039 --> 00:13:44,254
It was said that he would sometimes
work for thirty hours straight,

216
00:13:44,254 --> 00:13:45,890
and then sleep for twenty.

217
00:13:46,890 --> 00:13:48,259
He was deeply lonely.

218
00:13:48,259 --> 00:13:52,564
Few people in England shared his
language, his faith, or his food.

219
00:13:52,564 --> 00:13:56,412
Hardy admired him enormously,
but Hardy was a reserved man,

220
00:13:56,412 --> 00:14:01,304
uneasy with personal matters, and their
friendship lived almost entirely in

221
00:14:01,304 --> 00:14:02,086
mathematics.

222
00:14:02,086 --> 00:14:04,043
Letters from home grew scarce.

223
00:14:04,043 --> 00:14:08,739
Some of his wife's letters, it was
later said, never reached him at all.

224
00:14:09,739 --> 00:14:13,469
In the spring of 1917,
Ramanujan fell seriously ill.

225
00:14:13,469 --> 00:14:16,841
Doctors suspected
tuberculosis, then incurable.

226
00:14:16,841 --> 00:14:22,795
Some modern researchers believe he may
instead have suffered from a parasitic liver

227
00:14:22,795 --> 00:14:25,952
infection, picked up years earlier in India.

228
00:14:25,952 --> 00:14:29,467
Whatever its cause, the
illness never let him go.

229
00:14:30,467 --> 00:14:35,632
For the next two years, he moved between
hospitals and nursing homes: in Cambridge,

230
00:14:35,632 --> 00:14:38,743
in the Mendip Hills, in
Derbyshire, and in London.

231
00:14:38,743 --> 00:14:43,161
He was a difficult patient, homesick,
refusing food he could not trust,

232
00:14:43,161 --> 00:14:45,339
and sinking into a deep depression.

233
00:14:46,339 --> 00:14:52,192
In early 1918, at one of his lowest moments,
Ramanujan tried to take his own life.

234
00:14:52,192 --> 00:14:53,048
He survived.

235
00:14:53,048 --> 00:14:57,688
Hardy, it is said, intervened with
the authorities on his behalf.

236
00:15:00,188 --> 00:15:05,181
It was during this dark period that one
of the most famous stories in mathematics

237
00:15:05,181 --> 00:15:05,859
took place.

238
00:15:05,859 --> 00:15:10,852
Hardy came to visit his friend at a nursing
home in Putney, in south-west London.

239
00:15:11,852 --> 00:15:16,352
I remember once going to see him
when he was lying ill at Putney.

240
00:15:16,352 --> 00:15:20,090
I had ridden in taxi-cab
number seventeen twenty-nine,

241
00:15:20,090 --> 00:15:24,244
and remarked that the number
seemed to me rather a dull one,

242
00:15:24,244 --> 00:15:27,636
and that I hoped it was
not an unfavourable omen.

243
00:15:27,636 --> 00:15:30,959
No, he replied, it is a
very interesting number;

244
00:15:30,959 --> 00:15:36,775
it is the smallest number expressible as
the sum of two cubes in two different ways.

245
00:15:38,375 --> 00:15:40,450
One cubed, plus twelve cubed.

246
00:15:40,450 --> 00:15:42,381
Nine cubed, plus ten cubed.

247
00:15:42,381 --> 00:15:44,671
Both make seventeen twenty-nine.

248
00:15:44,671 --> 00:15:48,605
Even gravely ill, Ramanujan
saw numbers as old friends.

249
00:15:48,605 --> 00:15:54,400
As Littlewood once put it, every positive
integer was one of Ramanujan's personal

250
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:54,973
friends.

251
00:16:02,473 --> 00:16:06,013
Even as his body failed,
recognition finally came.

252
00:16:06,013 --> 00:16:11,820
On the second of May, 1918, Ramanujan was
elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of

253
00:16:11,820 --> 00:16:14,865
London, one of the youngest in its history,

254
00:16:14,865 --> 00:16:18,689
and only the second Indian
ever to receive the honour.

255
00:16:19,689 --> 00:16:24,763
In October of the same year, he became
the first Indian to be elected a Fellow of

256
00:16:24,763 --> 00:16:26,454
Trinity College, Cambridge.

257
00:16:26,454 --> 00:16:29,524
The news travelled to
India, and made him famous.

258
00:16:29,524 --> 00:16:34,786
The clerk from the Madras Port Trust now
stood among the most honoured scientists of

259
00:16:34,786 --> 00:16:35,350
his time.

260
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:38,646
In November 1918, the war ended.

261
00:16:38,646 --> 00:16:41,803
Ships could once again sail safely to India.

262
00:16:41,803 --> 00:16:47,542
His health had improved, a little, and
his doctors decided he was well enough to

263
00:16:47,542 --> 00:16:48,403
travel home.

264
00:16:55,903 --> 00:16:59,318
In late February 1919,
Ramanujan sailed for India.

265
00:16:59,318 --> 00:17:03,279
He arrived in Bombay in March,
and travelled on to Madras,

266
00:17:03,279 --> 00:17:05,669
where admirers waited to greet him.

267
00:17:05,669 --> 00:17:11,064
His wife Janaki joined him, and cared
for him through the months that followed.

268
00:17:12,064 --> 00:17:14,875
He was painfully thin, and often in pain.

269
00:17:14,875 --> 00:17:16,862
Yet he never stopped working.

270
00:17:16,862 --> 00:17:21,455
Lying in bed, he filled loose sheets
of paper with new mathematics.

271
00:17:21,455 --> 00:17:25,910
In January 1920, he wrote what
would be his last letter to Hardy.

272
00:17:26,910 --> 00:17:31,800
I am extremely sorry for not writing
you a single letter up to now.

273
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:37,932
I discovered very interesting functions
recently, which I call mock theta functions.

274
00:17:39,732 --> 00:17:41,143
Mock theta functions.

275
00:17:41,143 --> 00:17:43,695
Ramanujan described seventeen of them,

276
00:17:43,695 --> 00:17:47,994
with no definition: only examples,
and a few cryptic properties.

277
00:17:47,994 --> 00:17:53,637
It would take mathematicians more than
eighty years to understand what he had found.

278
00:17:54,637 --> 00:17:59,221
On the twenty-sixth of April,
1920, Srinivasa Ramanujan died.

279
00:17:59,221 --> 00:18:01,325
He was thirty-two years old.

280
00:18:06,325 --> 00:18:07,687
Hardy was devastated.

281
00:18:07,687 --> 00:18:10,736
He spent much of the rest
of his life studying,

282
00:18:10,736 --> 00:18:13,720
explaining, and championing
his friend's work.

283
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:14,303
He wrote:

284
00:18:15,303 --> 00:18:19,088
I owe more to him than to
anyone else in the world,

285
00:18:19,088 --> 00:18:25,172
with one exception, and my association
with him is the one romantic incident in my

286
00:18:25,172 --> 00:18:25,543
life.

287
00:18:33,543 --> 00:18:36,973
Ramanujan left behind nearly
four thousand results,

288
00:18:36,973 --> 00:18:39,798
almost all of them recorded without proof.

289
00:18:39,798 --> 00:18:43,564
For a century, mathematicians
have worked to prove them.

290
00:18:43,564 --> 00:18:47,128
The overwhelming majority
have turned out to be true.

291
00:18:48,128 --> 00:18:53,939
In 1976, the American mathematician George
Andrews was searching through a box of

292
00:18:53,939 --> 00:18:57,167
old papers in the library
of Trinity College.

293
00:18:57,167 --> 00:19:02,189
Inside, he found more than a hundred
pages in Ramanujan's handwriting,

294
00:19:02,189 --> 00:19:04,485
from the final year of his life.

295
00:19:04,485 --> 00:19:07,139
It became known as the lost notebook.

296
00:19:08,139 --> 00:19:10,147
And the mock theta functions?

297
00:19:10,147 --> 00:19:13,817
In 2002, a young Dutch
mathematician, Sander Zwegers,

298
00:19:13,817 --> 00:19:17,279
finally revealed the hidden
structure behind them.

299
00:19:17,279 --> 00:19:21,780
Today, they appear in places
Ramanujan could never have imagined,

300
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:24,619
including the mathematics of black holes,

301
00:19:24,619 --> 00:19:25,866
and string theory.

302
00:19:26,866 --> 00:19:29,766
His ideas live on in computer algorithms,

303
00:19:29,766 --> 00:19:33,445
in signal processing, and
in the design of networks.

304
00:19:33,445 --> 00:19:36,345
His name is attached to Ramanujan graphs,

305
00:19:36,345 --> 00:19:40,095
the Ramanujan tau function,
the Ramanujan conjecture,

306
00:19:40,095 --> 00:19:44,127
and to the Hardy-Ramanujan
number, seventeen twenty-nine.

307
00:19:45,127 --> 00:19:49,896
Hardy once rated mathematicians on
a scale from zero to one hundred,

308
00:19:49,896 --> 00:19:51,579
for pure natural talent.

309
00:19:51,579 --> 00:19:53,542
He gave himself twenty-five.

310
00:19:53,542 --> 00:19:54,874
Littlewood, thirty.

311
00:19:54,874 --> 00:19:57,118
The great David Hilbert, eighty.

312
00:19:57,118 --> 00:19:59,292
Ramanujan, he gave one hundred.

313
00:20:00,292 --> 00:20:03,754
In India, his birthday, the
twenty-second of December,

314
00:20:03,754 --> 00:20:06,447
is celebrated as National Mathematics Day.

315
00:20:06,447 --> 00:20:09,139
His house in Kumbakonam welcomes visitors,

316
00:20:09,139 --> 00:20:12,665
and students around the world
still open his notebooks,

317
00:20:12,665 --> 00:20:14,332
searching for what he saw.

318
00:20:15,332 --> 00:20:20,413
Srinivasa Ramanujan had no formal training,
few resources, and very little time.

319
00:20:20,413 --> 00:20:24,669
He came from a small temple town,
with a borrowed book and a slate.

320
00:20:24,669 --> 00:20:26,892
He lived for just thirty-two years.

321
00:20:26,892 --> 00:20:31,655
More than a century later, the world
is still catching up with what he saw.

